    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1316">

                <pb facs="1410" n="1396" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1396"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/7–744: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stewart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-07T12:00:00-06:00">July 7,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 5:54 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>429. The Embassy learned yesterday afternoon that Colonel Lindberg<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d1316-f1"/> as President of the Junta de Control de Precios y de
                    Comercio had signed and circulated an order as [<hi rendition="#italic">to</hi>]
                    the proprietors of all business houses in Managua stating that if any citizen
                    closed the doors of his establishment the Government would appoint an
                    interventor to assume charge of it for the purpose of selling off the stock at
                    cost and he would be denied the right again to engage in business. If a
                    foreigner closed the doors of his place of business he would be subject to
                    expulsion from the country.</p>
                <p>Colonel Lindberg is criticized for his action in this morning’s <hi
                        rendition="#italic">La Prensa</hi> not only because constitutional
                    guarantees, notably article 60 it is argued, have been infringed but because an
                    alien has intervened in the internal affairs of Nicaragua.</p>
                <p>Colonel Lindberg has informed me that he signed the order at the request of
                    President Somoza.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stewart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1316-f1" n="7" xml:id="d1316-f1-content">
                <p>Col. Irving A. Lindberg, Collector General of Customs and High Commissioner in
                    Nicaragua; an American citizen.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
